      "dom_id": "question-46833",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x-2}{2x-\\sqrt{8x}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=2$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x-2}{2x-\\sqrt{8x}} & =\\frac{2-2}{2(2)-\\sqrt{8(2)}}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{x-2}{2x-\\sqrt{8x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{(2x-\\sqrt{8x})(2x+\\sqrt{8x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{(2x)^{2}-{(\\sqrt{8x})}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{4{x}^{2}-8x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{(x-2)(2x+\\sqrt{8x})}{4x(x-2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{2x+\\sqrt{8x}}{4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2)+\\sqrt{8(2)}}{4(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+4}{8} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
